.TH IMPORT 4
.SH NAME
import \- mount another machine's tree here
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import
[
.B -a
|
.B -b
]
.I host mountpoint
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Import
dials
.BI tcp! host !9fs,
where that machine's
.IR listen (8)
runs
.IR exportfs (4),
posts the stream as
.BI /srv/ host,
and mounts it at
.IR mountpoint .
.B -a
and
.B -b
are the mount's order, after or before what is already there.
It is
.IR srv (4)
and
.B mount
in one:
.IP
.EX
% import big /n/big
% ls /n/big/proc
.EE
Before anything is posted the handshake runs, through
.IR factotum (4):
this session proves itself as the user
.B /env/user
in
.BR /env/dom ,
and the host proves itself by the
.B key=
on its record in
.BR /lib/ndb/local .
A host with no key cannot be dialled, and there is no falling back to the
clear; what is posted is the sealed stream.
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/import/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR srv (4),
.IR exportfs (4),
.IR bind (1)
.SH BUGS
Plan 9's
.I import
names the far tree as well and asks for it by an attach name; this wire
has none, so the far side serves what its service script names and this
end takes all of it.
A
.B bind
after the mount picks the part.
